e building, which is the deregulation side of the question. But one deregulation law can't show rung 5's \"at most\" (that she'd do nothing beyond cutting rules). Under the 2026-09-26 refinement, a law like this is `on-question` but only `direction-only`. Her own words (\"get government out of the way, lower costs\") don't rule out subsidies or tax breaks either, so rung 4 and rung 5 can't be told apart.\n\n**Passages coded (all `on-question`, `direction-only`, `in-term`):**\n- **Two press releases** (2025-09-17 and 2026-01-09): her own words, coded `statement-other`.\n- **Clerk roll call 12:** her Yea vote, with the tally. The vote isn't near-unanimous (147 against out of 410), and the bill covers one subject.\n- **Congress.gov bill page:** her sponsor line, with the bill's operative section quoted as the provision.\n\n**Sources still needed (`needs_source`):** her own words or record on housing subsidies and tax credits, which would separate rung 4 from rung 5, and on public housing or binding rules such as rent caps.\n\n**Quotes:** I listed two of her statements about the Act. Both name a specific bill as the means, so I tiered them `lever` and `lever-named`, and marked both quotable.","ttft_ms":14032,"type":"result","duration_ms":36565,"uuid":"140d4fa6-b458-4876-adf4-1f9b295d6c75","ttft_stream_ms":1657,"time_to_request_ms":77,"first_content_frame_ms":1657,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
